A4: E-MAIL SPAM FILTERING USING TEXT CLASSIFICATION (Naive Bayes)

AIM: Train a classifier on labeled e-mails and predict whether new mail is spam or ham.

CODE EXPLAINED
1. Imports
   CountVectorizer (text -> word-count vectors), train_test_split, MultinomialNB (the classifier), metrics.

2. data = [(text, label), ...]
   16 small labeled e-mails: 8 "spam", 8 "ham" (ham = not spam). In a real exam you could load a CSV instead.

3. texts = [...]; labels = [...]
   Splits the data into the list of mails (X side) and list of labels (y side).

4. vectorizer = CountVectorizer(lowercase=True, stop_words="english")
   X = vectorizer.fit_transform(texts)
   fit builds the vocabulary from all mails; transform converts each mail into a vector of word counts (bag of words). Stop words are ignored. X is a sparse matrix: rows = mails, columns = words.

5. train_test_split(X, labels, test_size=0.25, random_state=42, stratify=labels)
   75% train, 25% test. random_state=42 makes the split repeatable. stratify keeps equal spam/ham ratio in both parts.

6. model = MultinomialNB(); model.fit(X_train, y_train)
   Training: for each word it learns P(word | spam) and P(word | ham), plus class priors.

7. y_pred = model.predict(X_test)
   Predicts for each test mail the class with the highest probability.

8. accuracy_score, confusion_matrix, classification_report
   Accuracy = correct / total. Confusion matrix rows = actual, columns = predicted (order ham, spam). Report shows precision, recall, F1 per class.

9. New mails: vectorizer.transform(new_mails)
   IMPORTANT: use transform (not fit_transform) so new mails use the same vocabulary as training. Then predict and print SPAM/HAM.

PIPELINE: text -> bag of words -> train/test split -> train NB -> predict -> evaluate.

VIVA Q&A
Q: What is text classification?
A: Assigning a document to a predefined category using a model trained on labeled examples. It is supervised learning.

Q: What is Naive Bayes?
A: Probabilistic classifier using Bayes theorem: P(class|words) proportional to P(class) x product of P(word|class).

Q: Why "naive"?
A: It assumes words are independent given the class. Untrue, but works well in practice.

Q: Why MultinomialNB?
A: It models word counts, so it suits bag-of-words features.

Q: What does CountVectorizer do?
A: Builds the vocabulary and converts each text into a vector of word counts.

Q: What is bag of words?
A: Text as word counts, ignoring order.

Q: Why fit on training but only transform on new data?
A: The model only knows the training vocabulary; refitting would change the feature columns.

Q: Why stratify in train_test_split?
A: Keeps the class ratio equal in train and test sets.

Q: Precision, recall, F1?
A: Precision = TP/(TP+FP); recall = TP/(TP+FN); F1 = harmonic mean of both.

Q: Why is accuracy only 75%?
A: Dataset is tiny (4 test mails, one wrong = 25%). Real datasets (SMS Spam Collection) give about 97%+.

Q: What is Laplace smoothing?
A: Adds 1 (alpha=1) to every word count so unseen words don't give probability 0.

Q: Which error is worse, false positive or false negative?
A: False positive (real mail marked spam), since the user may miss important mail.

Q: Other algorithms for this task?
A: SVM, Logistic Regression, KNN, Decision Tree, Random Forest.

Q: How would you improve the filter?
A: More data, TF-IDF features, n-grams, stemming, tuning alpha.

